syntax:
C++ syntax demonstrating RVO/NRVO:

```c++
class MyClass {
public:
  MyClass(int val) : value(val) {}
  MyClass(const MyClass& other) { value = other.value; std::cout << "Copy constructor called\n"; }
  MyClass(MyClass&& other) noexcept { value = other.value; std::cout << "Move constructor called\n"; }
  ~MyClass() { std::cout << "Destructor called\n"; }
  int value;
};

MyClass createObject(int val) {
  MyClass obj(val);
  return obj; // RVO likely here
}

MyClass createObject2(int val) {
  MyClass obj(val);
  return std::move(obj); // Move constructor might be used if RVO is not applied
}

int main() {
  MyClass myObj = createObject(10);
  MyClass myObj2 = createObject2(10);
  return 0;
}
```

Note:  The presence of `std::cout` statements in the constructors and destructor shows whether a copy or move constructor is actually called (or if the compiler has optimized away the calls).  Compile with high optimization flags (-O2 or -O3 for GCC/Clang) to see the impact of RVO/NRVO.  Without these optimization flags, the copy or move constructors are likely to be called.
